 ### [Multiresolution with Octree Bricking](6106-multiresolution-with-octree-bricking.md)
-Multiresolution with Octree Bricking is a data representation and rendering technique for large volumetric datasets that provides high-fidelity results in regions of interest while maintaining the context of the whole volume at a lower resolution [6106-multiresolution-with-octree-bricking]. It sets itself apart from siblings like [Ray-marching reference (min-max octree accelerated)](1554-ray-marching-reference-min-max-octree-accelerated.md) by explicitly structuring the volume into hierarchical "bricks" (sub-volumes) rather than just using occupancy flags for pruning, allowing for adaptive resolution based on spatial locality [6106-multiresolution-with-octree-bricking].
+Multiresolution with Octree Bricking encodes volumetric data at varying resolutions into a spatial octree hierarchy to enable adaptive, high-fidelity rendering of large datasets [6106-multiresolution-with-octree-bricking]. It solves the problem of limited memory and rendering performance by allowing the system to selectively query and render nodes at resolutions appropriate to their distance from the viewer or their visual importance [6106-multiresolution-with-octree-bricking]. Unlike sibling methods that may use fixed brick sizes or hardware-accelerated BVH traversal for static structures, this method explicitly links octree depth to resolution levels, subsampling children to form parent nodes, and relies on a software-managed out-of-core loading strategy for datasets exceeding texture memory [6106-multiresolution-with-octree-bricking].
 
