+#### Block-based GPU Volume Rendering with Acceleration
+- **Definition:** An efficient GPU rendering algorithm for large CT datasets that subdivides the volume into uniform blocks and accelerates rendering by combining early ray termination, empty-space skipping, and visibility culling to render only visible parts [S1, unofficial].
+- **Steps:** 1. Subdivide the volume dataset into uniform-sized blocks. 2. Apply visibility culling to identify visible blocks. 3. Render visible blocks using early ray termination and empty-space skipping to skip transparent/empty regions [S1, unofficial].
+- **Performance:** Achieved 3-4 frames per second on a 512 × 304 × 1878 dimension dataset (visible female) using a Pentium 4 2.4 GHz PC with an NVIDIA Geforce 6600 (256 MB video memory) [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of hierarchical data structures for volume rendering emerged in the early 2000s as a response to the memory and computational demands of large volumetric datasets. Early work focused on octrees and min-max structures to enable empty space skipping [1554-ray-marching-reference-min-max-octree-accelerated]. The introduction of GPU hardware accelerated the adoption of block-based rendering, where volumes are subdivided into bricks to optimize texture cache usage [4263-brick-size-and-octree-limit-tuning]. More recent developments leverage hardware ray-tracing cores (RTX) to traverse these hierarchical structures more efficiently, moving traversal logic from software shaders to dedicated hardware [2577-exabricks-rendering-with-rtx-bvh]. These methods are widely used in medical imaging (e.g., CT datasets) and scientific visualization where large, multi-resolution datasets are common [4950-residency-octree][4263-brick-size-and-octree-limit-tuning].
+The concept of hierarchical data structures for volume rendering emerged to address the computational cost of ray marching through dense voxel grids. Early work focused on octrees and bricked grids to enable empty space skipping. The specific technique of using min-max octrees for isosurface extraction was detailed in works such as [1554-ray-marching-reference-min-max-octree-accelerated]. The use of hardware-accelerated BVHs for adaptive block-refined data represents a more recent development leveraging RTX cores [2577-exabricks-rendering-with-rtx-bvh]. Out-of-core rendering strategies, such as the Residency Octree, were developed to handle datasets exceeding GPU memory limits [4950-residency-octree].
 
